### 7.3 Real x86_64 IOL lab

1. After the one-download path has reached the GUI, stage one owner-supplied,
   legally held x86_64 IOL image as a test input; it is not part of the archive
   or prerequisite claim. Upload/register it through the rendered GUI.
2. Create a two-node x86_64 IOL lab, give each node `ram: 1024`, link them,
   save it, and start it. Never use `ram: 256`; it can report running while IOS
   is wedged by allocation failures.
3. Wait for real console readiness, not merely node `running`. Configure
   addresses and perform bidirectional traffic. Console connections replay a
   ring buffer, so use the last complete ping summary. Wake IOS consoles,
   disable pagination before long output, and wait for the final prompt.
4. Save console transcripts and a final no-loss-or-documented-first-ARP-loss
   ping result. Confirm the hello/capability UI remains honest: x86_64 is
   usable, i386 is absent/disabled, and no arm64-native IOL support is implied.
5. If browser-equivalent automation is used for evidence, first obtain the
   session cookie and send the matching `Origin` on every WebSocket route. A
