   1: # M4 handoff â€” Apple Silicon macOS track
   2: 
   3: Updated 2026-08-14, end of the M4 session. Read this **before**
   4: `docs/macos-m3-handoff.md` â€” this supersedes it for anything M4 changed;
   5: M1-M3's own findings are still current and not repeated here.
   6: 
   7: Branch: **`luna/macos-m4-runtime`**, off `luna/macos-m3-ux`, in worktree
   8: `J:\Claude code\iolab-m4-wt`. **Not merged anywhere.**
   9: 
  10: ---
  11: 
  12: ## 1. Status: PARTIAL, not PASS â€” read `docs/macos-m4-result.md` in full
  13: 
  14: Short version: items 1 (VPCS/IOL) and 6 (soak+power) are genuinely proven
  15: on real Apple Silicon hardware. Items 2 and 7 have real fixes that are
  16: unit-tested but not hardware-reconfirmed. Items 3, 4, 5, 8 were never
  17: attempted this session. Eleven real defects were found and fixed (two of
  18: them shared product code, already merged to `main` and hardware-verified
  19: independently of this branch); one more is filed but unresolved. Full
  20: per-item table and defect list in `docs/macos-m4-result.md`.
  21: 
  22: **Do not treat M4 as closed.** The owner explicitly chose to move on to M5
  23: with this state â€” that's a scope call the owner is entitled to make, but a
  24: future session should not assume "M4 handoff exists" means "M4 passed."
  25: 
  26: ---
  27: 
  28: ## 2. The tarball-overwrite trap â€” the single most important thing to know
  29: 
  30: `hardware-m4.sh`'s (and both sub-scripts') default `--tarball` is the
  31: plain M4-scope build with **no tool packs**. Running the harness without
  32: an explicit override silently reinstalls that plain build over any
  33: fixed/richer build already on the VM. This happened twice this session and
  34: each time looked exactly like a regression until traced back to the
  35: harness's own default. If you're testing anything touching `pc`/`aaa`/
  36: `webserver`/etc. node kinds, pass
  37: `--tarball ~/iolbox-m0/iolbox-server-v0.5.3-netprobe-cgroupfd-fix.tar.gz`
  38: (or a newer one, if it exists â€” check `~/iolbox-m0/*.tar.gz` and compare
  39: mtimes/hashes) every single time, and re-verify the running supervisor's
  40: version string after any `launcher_start` before trusting pack behavior.
  41: 
  42: ---
  43: 
  44: ## 3. Sub-test scripts, for iterating on one item without the full 8-item run
  45: 
  46: - `packaging/macos/tests/hardware-m4-startstop.sh` â€” items covering forced
  47:   launcher kill / forced VM stop / recovery, in isolation.
  48: - `packaging/macos/tests/hardware-m4-power.sh` â€” item 6 (soak+power audit)
  49:   in isolation, ~11 minutes wall clock.
  50: 
  51: Both work by sourcing everything in `hardware-m4.sh` *except* its own
  52: trailing `main "$@"` line, so they reuse the exact same
  53: preflight/sentinel/cleanup functions. **If you edit `hardware-m4.sh`,
  54: check whether either sub-script has its own inline copy of the changed
  55: line** â€” `hardware-m4-power.sh` had a stale inline copy of the
  56: `soak-seal-verify` command that silently kept the old `-args` bug alive
  57: for a full extra ~11-minute run after the shared script was already fixed,
  58: because the sub-script's OWN copy wasn't touched. Prefer having sub-scripts
  59: call into sourced functions rather than inline-copying lines from
  60: `hardware-m4.sh`, if extending this pattern further.
  61: 
  62: ---
  63: 
  64: ## 4. Two shared-code fixes landed on `main`, unrelated to this branch
  65: 
  66: Both hardware-verified, both affect every deployment target (not
  67: Mac-specific), both found while testing M4's VPCS/tool-pack-adjacent scope:
  68: 
  69: - `main@e792c32` â€” native install target was missing all tool-pack
  70:   binaries (netprobe, aaa, webserver, httpclient, syslog, netsvc,
  71:   secbench).
  72: - `main@df24ab1` (merge `4f7643c`) â€” the native cgroup-placement fallback
  73:   used a path-based `cgroup.procs` write that's invisible once wrapped in
  74:   `ip netns exec` (fresh mount namespace hides the parent's cgroup2 view).
  75:   Fixed to use an inherited fd instead. This is the fix that actually made
  76:   netprobe/AAA/etc. boot; the binaries-shipped fix alone wasn't sufficient.
  77: 
  78: If a future session sees tool-pack nodes failing with `runtime does not
  79: support ... nodes` again, check `git log main` for whether these two
  80: commits are still present before re-diagnosing from scratch â€” and check
  81: Â§2's tarball trap first, since that's the far more likely cause of an
  82: apparent regression.
  83: 
  84: ---
  85: 
  86: ## 5. Environment as it actually is now
  87: 
  88: See `docs/macos-m4-result.md` Â§5 for the full table (host, key path,
  89: current supervisor build string, VM inventory). One item worth repeating
  90: here: the SSH key used throughout this session is a workspace-local copy
  91: at `.m4-ssh/iolbox_mac_m0` (gitignored), not `~/.ssh/iolbox_mac_m0`
  92: directly â€” the sandboxed environment that did most of this work couldn't
  93: read the real path even with directory grants. If working from an
  94: unsandboxed environment, the real `~/.ssh/iolbox_mac_m0` works fine
  95: directly and the workspace-local copy isn't needed.
  96: 
  97: ---
  98: 
  99: ## 6. Process notes carried forward + new ones from this session
 100: 
 101: - Same `codex exec` stdin-hang and sandbox-write gotchas as before (see
 102:   M3 handoff Â§7) â€” unchanged.
 103: - **Never assume a background/detached hardware run's failure is the
 104:   "real" answer without checking whether the harness itself has a bug** â€”
 105:   of the defects found this session, more were in the test harness
 106:   (`-args` misuse, seal-ordering, regex too narrow, timeout too short,
 107:   console read race) than in the product. Read the actual stdout/stderr of
 108:   a failing `record_command`, not just the top-level `die()` message,
 109:   before concluding a hardware defect.
 110: - A Lima VM's own `lima.yaml` can get rewritten in a different YAML style
 111:   (block sequence instead of flow) by Lima's own tooling after a
 112:   stop/start cycle â€” a launcher that hand-parses this file (rather than
 113:   using a real YAML library) needs to tolerate both styles for fields it
 114:   itself wrote in only one style at VM-creation time. This bit the
 115:   start/stop recovery test specifically, but would affect any code path
 116:   that re-inspects an already-running VM's port-forward contract.
