===== PROVIDERS =====
   1: # Runtime providers (contract)
   2: 
   3: A **provider** hosts the Linux environment that runs the supervisor + IOL + VPCS.
   4: The Windows-side app (Rust core in Tauri) selects and drives one. The provider
   5: interface is deliberately tiny so new backends are cheap.
   6: 
   7: ## Interface (Rust trait, conceptual)
   8: 
   9: ```rust
  10: trait Provider {
  11:     fn id() -> &'static str;                 // "vmware" | "wsl2" | "remote" | "qemu"
  12:     fn detect() -> Detection;                // available? preferred? why/why not
  13:     fn provision(&self, appliance: &Path) -> Result<()>;  // one-time import/setup
  14:     fn start(&self) -> Result<Endpoint>;     // boot runtime, return control endpoint
  15:     fn stop(&self) -> Result<()>;
  16:     fn endpoint(&self) -> Option<Endpoint>;  // { host, controlPort } on Windows localhost
  17:     fn sync_image(&self, local: &Path) -> Result<String>; // copy image into runtime, return remote path
  18:     fn health(&self) -> Health;
  19: }
  20: ```
  21: 
  22: `Endpoint` is always reachable from Windows as `host:port` (usually
  23: `127.0.0.1:4000`). Everything above the provider (GUI, supervisor protocol, lab
  24: format, consoles, capture) is identical regardless of which provider is active.
  25: 
  26: ## Selection & preflight (first run)
  27: 
  28: Detect and rank; present a plain-English choice, never auto-flip system features.
  29: 
  30: | Provider | `detect()` logic | Rank |
  31: |---|---|---|
  32: | `vmware` | `vmrun.exe` found (Workstation or Player) | **1 on VMware machines** |
  33: | `wsl2` | `wsl.exe` present AND Hyper-V/VMP already enabled | 1 if enabled, else offered-with-warning |
  34: | `remote` | user-configured host reachable | manual |
  35: | `qemu` | always (bundled) | last resort |
  36: 
  37: **Critical rule:** never enable the Windows Hypervisor Platform / Hyper-V
  38: automatically â€” it degrades VMware Workstation and kills nested virtualization.
  39: If WSL2 would require enabling it, mark WSL2 "requires a Windows feature that will
  40: affect VMware" and default to `vmware`.
  41: 
  42: ## vmware (primary)
  43: 
  44: - Ship a small appliance (`iolbox-appliance.vmx` + `.vmdk`, Debian-slim + kernel +
  45:   supervisor autostart). 2 vCPU / 4 GB RAM (IOL nodes default to 1 GB each),
  46:   **two NICs**: host-only (control/GUI/console/capture) + NAT (the NAT node's
  47:   MASQUERADE outbound path). Same shape as the proven iolbox-rt reference VM.
  48: - Lifecycle: `vmrun -T ws start <vmx> nogui` / `stop`. Player: `-T player`.
  49: - Endpoint discovery: `vmrun getGuestIPAddress <vmx> -wait` (open-vm-tools is
  50:   in the image), or match the appliance's fixed host-only MAC
  51:   `00:50:56:3f:ab:01` in `C:\ProgramData\VMware\vmnetdhcp.leases`. The
  52:   host-only address is a DHCP lease â€” subnets differ per Workstation install,
  53:   so no IP is baked in. GUI/WS bridge at `http://<ip>:4001`.
  54: - Image sync: the GUI's image upload (`POST /api/upload/image` on :4001) â€”
  55:   no vmrun guest-auth or shared folders needed.
  56: - **De-risked**: this is the same `vmrun`-managed-VM pattern already used daily for
  57:   the PNetLab gate VMs.
  58: 
  59: ## wsl2
  60: 
  61: - `wsl --import iolbox <dir> iolbox-rootfs.tar` (first run). No Store distro needed.
  62: - Endpoint: WSL2 localhost forwarding â†’ `127.0.0.1:4000` directly.
  63: - Image sync: images live on a Windows path visible at `/mnt/...`, or copied in.
  64: - Fastest cold start, smallest footprint. Chosen only when Hyper-V already on.
  65: 
  66: ## remote
  67: 
  68: - User supplies `ssh user@host`. App scps the supervisor binary + starts it,
  69:   tunnels control/console/capture ports over SSH. Also the CI/headless mode.
  70: 
  71: ## qemu (compatibility)
  72: 
  73: - Bundled `qemu-system-x86_64` + tiny kernel/initrd running the supervisor, TCG
  74:   (no hypervisor). Slow (IOL idle spin hurts), but conflicts with nothing.
  75: - Endpoint: user-mode net with hostfwd for control/console/capture ports.
  76: 
  77: ## Appliance / rootfs build
  78: 
  79: Both the VMware appliance and the WSL tar come from **one** Debian-slim rootfs:
  80: - glibc + `libc6:i386` (32-bit IOL), minimal userspace + what the supervisor
  81:   shells out to at runtime: `sudo`, `iproute2`, `procps` (sysctl), `iptables`
  82:   (NAT node)
  83: - `/opt/iolbox/supervisor` (Go binary, GUI embedded â€” build via
  84:   `build-release.sh`) started by systemd on boot with the full proven flag set
  85:   (`-ws-addr 0.0.0.0:4001`, `-console-bind`/`-capture-bind 0.0.0.0`, labs/run
  86:   dirs)
  87: - hostname pinned to `iolbox` (`/etc/hostname` + `/etc/hosts` + `wsl.conf`) â€”
  88:   the iourc is keyed to it and `sudo` stalls without the hosts line
  89: - iourc generated on first boot from the runtime's own hostid
  90: - default route on the NAT NIC (the NAT node needs outbound; the old
  91:   no-default-route posture is retired â€” see runtime/README.md)
  92: 
  93: Build scripts live in `runtime/`. Reproducible; nothing Cisco is ever baked in.
===== THIRD PARTY =====
   1: # Third-party software distributed with iolbox
   2: 
   3: iolbox bundles the following third-party components. Their licenses are their
   4: own; this file records what we ship, from where, pinned to an exact version and
   5: checksum, and how we comply with each license.
   6: 
   7: ---
   8: 
   9: ## QEMU (Windows build) â€” the `qemu` compatibility backend
  10: 
  11: The Windows launcher (`tools/iolab-launcher`) ships a trimmed copy of QEMU so the
  12: `qemu` backend (software-emulated TCG, see `docs/providers.md` and
  13: `runtime/qemu-compat.md`) works on any Windows machine with zero prerequisites.
  14: 
  15: ### Pinned source
  16: 
  17: | Field | Value |
  18: |---|---|
  19: | Upstream | Stefan Weil's official QEMU-for-Windows builds â€” <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/> |
  20: | File | `qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe` |
  21: | Full URL | <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe> |
  22: | QEMU version | 11.0.0 release build (reports `11.0.50 (v11.0.0-12631-g54e84cdc7a)`) |
  23: | Installer size | 190 MB (198,760,632 bytes) |
  24: | **SHA-256 (installer, verified by download)** | `a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef` |
  25: 
  26: `qemu.weilnetz.de` is the canonical upstream-linked Windows build (referenced
  27: from qemu.org's download page). Note: recent installers are Authenticode-signed
  28: with an **expired certificate** â€” Windows SmartScreen may warn on the installer.
  29: This does not affect the extracted binaries we redistribute (we extract, we do
  30: not run the installer on user machines).
  31: 
  32: ### How the redistributable is produced (extraction, not install)
  33: 
  34: The upstream `.exe` is an NSIS installer. We do **not** run it on user machines;
  35: we extract it once and ship a trimmed tree:
  36: 
  37: ```sh
  38: # 1. verify the pinned installer
  39: sha256sum qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
  40: #   -> a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef
  41: 
  42: # 2. extract with 7-Zip (NSIS archive)
  43: "C:\Program Files\7-Zip\7z.exe" x -oqemu-extract qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
  44: 
  45: # 3. trim to the headless x86_64 target:
  46: #    - keep  qemu-system-x86_64.exe  + ALL *.dll (the runtime DLL set)
  47: #    - keep  share/keymaps/  and the x86/i386 firmware blobs
  48: #            (bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*/multiboot*/pvh,
  49: #             *-virtio.rom + pxe-*.rom, edk2-i386/x86_64 *.fd)
  50: #    - drop  the other 55 qemu-system-*.exe targets, share/doc, and the
  51: #            non-x86 firmware (openbios-*, edk2-aarch64/arm, dtb, ...)
  52: ```
  53: 
  54: Result: a `qemu/` directory of ~175 MB. Verified standalone on a clean box:
  55: `qemu-system-x86_64.exe -version` and `-accel help` (lists `tcg` + `whpx`) both
  56: run, so the DLL set is complete.
  57: 
  58: ### Layout shipped next to the launcher exe
  59: 
  60: ```
  61: iolbox-launcher.exe
  62: iolbox-disk.qcow2            (the runtime disk â€” a separate release asset, see below)
  63: qemu/
  64:   qemu-system-x86_64.exe
  65:   *.dll                     (the runtime DLLs)
  66:   share/
  67:     keymaps/
  68:     bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*.bin, ...
  69: ```
  70: 
  71: The launcher looks for `qemu/qemu-system-x86_64.exe` and `iolbox-disk.qcow2`
  72: relative to its own exe (`--qemu` / `--disk` override for dev). QEMU resolves its
  73: BIOS/keymap data from the `share/` dir next to its exe automatically.
  74: 
  75: The `qemu/` directory and `*.qcow2` are **git-ignored** (see
  76: `tools/iolab-launcher/.gitignore`) â€” they are release-time assets staged into the
  77: bundle, never committed to the repo.
  78: 
  79: ### License / GPL compliance
  80: 
  81: QEMU is licensed under the **GNU GPL v2** (with some components under LGPL v2.1
  82: and other compatible licenses). We redistribute unmodified upstream binaries.
  83: 
  84: - The upstream binaries carry their license texts (`COPYING`, `COPYING.LIB`) in
  85:   the installer; include a copy of `COPYING`/`COPYING.LIB` in the shipped `qemu/`
  86:   directory (or link to them from the release notes) when assembling a release.
  87: - **Written offer for source:** the corresponding source for this exact build is
  88:   published by the upstream packager at the QEMU source page,
  89:   <https://www.qemu.org/download/#source> (release 11.0.0), mirrored from
  90:   <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/>. iolbox distributes QEMU **unmodified**; the source
  91:   offer therefore points at upstream. Include this URL in the release notes /
  92:   About page alongside the version above so the offer travels with the binary.
  93: 
  94: ### The iolbox runtime disk (`iolbox-disk.qcow2`)
  95: 
  96: Not third-party â€” it is built from `runtime/` (Debian-slim rootfs + the iolbox
  97: supervisor) by `runtime/pack-qemu.sh`. It contains **no Cisco software**; IOL
  98: images are supplied by the user at runtime via the GUI. Debian's own components
  99: inside the rootfs are under their respective licenses (Debian is DFSG-free);
 100: this is the same rootfs the WSL2 and VMware artifacts ship, already covered by
 101: the runtime build.

